  {
    "episode_id": 72620,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72620.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 5,
    "turn_text": "Since 1970, voters in Harlem have sent Congressman Charles Rangel to represent them in Congress. Now, he's in a tough battle for re-election in a newly-redrawn congressional district with a Latino majority. I'm John Rudolph, executive producer of Feed In Two Worlds, a project of the Center for New York City Affairs at the New School. 55% of the voters in New York's 13th congressional district are Hispanic. This is one of the main challenges facing Congressman Charles Rangel in his re-election bid. Rangel was one of the founders of the Congressional Black Caucus, and he's been a leading voice for African Americans in Congress. On Tuesday, June 26th, Rangel faces four challengers in New York's Democratic primary. Congressman Rangel, welcome to the feet and twirls podcast. Thank you for joining us. Congressman Rangel, the race in your district seems to be as much about demographics as it is about politics. Since the 1940s, your current district has been represented in Congress by an African-American, but in the newly redrawn district, the majority has shifted from African-American to Hispanic. Wouldn't it be appropriate for the district to be represented in Congress by a Hispanic person?",
