    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 72620,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72620.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 14,
    "turn_text": "First of all, my leadership on the immigration issue goes to the first time that I saw in my district, newcomers. They were different from the Italians and the Irish and the Jewish people. But what an exciting opportunity it was for me to see the educational process, the school board process, to see Lenaris become the very first Dominican to get elected to public office. And so it's been just a joy working to make this equitable. And the faucets that are involved in this hypocritical immigration system are to say that people who come to the United States with the same vision as other people who are really constructively invited since nobody that hired them was indicted and that a whole economy as relates to hotel, hospitality, industry agriculture, so economically dependent on these people here, that we're not going to even think about getting rid of 11 million. And the Dream Act is a classic example of hypocrisy where we have invested all of this education and young aggressive people that love and know only one country that we have been working here in the progressive caucus that panic and the black caucus to persuade the president that there's a lot of things he can do that are being blocked up by the senate and by the republicans and this was one of them and so i just think It makes sense, and I don't see any downside at all. The more people complain that the president acted outside of his authority, the more I think that decent people would say it was the right thing to do.",
